WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:02.400
-Our next guest
is a very funny comedian.

00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:04.801
His comedy special
"Slow &amp; Steady"

00:00:04.801 --> 00:00:06.200
is streaming now on YouTube.

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:07.634
Let's take a look.

00:00:07.634 --> 00:00:09.801
-Was a difficult few months

00:00:09.801 --> 00:00:13.434
after the Dahmer show
came out on Netflix.

00:00:13.434 --> 00:00:15.667
[ Laughter ]

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:25.601
I was getting texts and e-mails

00:00:25.601 --> 00:00:30.100
from people I haven't heard from
in over a decade saying that,

00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:34.033
"The guy on Dahmer's show
looks and sounds like you."

00:00:34.033 --> 00:00:37.434
[ Laughter ]

00:00:37.434 --> 00:00:40.367
Couple kids on the street
even said,

00:00:40.367 --> 00:00:42.100
"Hey, Dahmer."

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:43.834
[ Laughter ]

00:00:43.834 --> 00:00:45.968
I'll be honest,

00:00:45.968 --> 00:00:52.868
if I did even half of the stuff
that Jeffrey Dahmer did,

00:00:52.868 --> 00:00:55.467
my dad would be so pissed off.

00:00:55.467 --> 00:00:56.968
[ Laughter ]

00:00:56.968 --> 00:01:00.067
-Please welcome back to the show
the one, the only Joe Pera.

00:01:00.067 --> 00:01:02.200
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:09.601
♪♪

00:01:09.601 --> 00:01:17.000
♪♪

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:20.300
Welcome back, Joe.
-Thank you very much.

00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:23.367
Hey, Fred, can I come
to your Thousands hair salon?

00:01:23.367 --> 00:01:24.734
-Yes.
-'Cause I could use

00:01:24.734 --> 00:01:26.567
about a thousand more hairs.

00:01:26.567 --> 00:01:30.100
[ Laughter ]

00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:33.501
They combed it over real good
backstage, but, yeah.

00:01:33.501 --> 00:01:35.367
-So, Joe, it's so great --

00:01:35.367 --> 00:01:39.701
Again, your ability to do,
you know, banter.

00:01:39.701 --> 00:01:41.467
[ Laughter ]

00:01:41.467 --> 00:01:42.534
You're back in New York City.

00:01:42.534 --> 00:01:44.167
You've been on tour
for over a year.

00:01:44.167 --> 00:01:46.634
How is it to be back
in New York?

00:01:46.634 --> 00:01:48.968
-It's really nice
to be back in New York,

00:01:48.968 --> 00:01:51.634
spend some quality time
with my side pieces.

00:01:51.634 --> 00:01:53.634
-Uh-huh.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:53.634 --> 00:01:55.334
-It's been tough.

00:01:55.334 --> 00:01:56.834
I've been mailing them

00:01:56.834 --> 00:01:59.367
$20 Best Buy gift cards
from the road.

00:01:59.367 --> 00:02:03.167
But that can only last
for so long.

00:02:03.167 --> 00:02:07.167
I don't want to brag,
but I've been buying printer ink

00:02:07.167 --> 00:02:10.767
and Nintendo Switch games for
women in three different states.

00:02:10.767 --> 00:02:12.801
-Wow.

00:02:12.801 --> 00:02:15.033
-Just -- Just -- Just a joke.

00:02:15.033 --> 00:02:16.634
-Oh, that's just a joke.

00:02:16.634 --> 00:02:18.567
[ Laughter ]

00:02:20.601 --> 00:02:24.234
Did you learn things about the
world while you were touring?

00:02:24.234 --> 00:02:26.200
I mean, you went
so many interesting places.

00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:28.767
-Yeah, it was
really enlightening.

00:02:28.767 --> 00:02:32.067
I mean, did you know
that in Ireland,

00:02:32.067 --> 00:02:35.801
they don't have
Bluetooth technology?

00:02:35.801 --> 00:02:38.234
That's true, and in Australia,

00:02:38.234 --> 00:02:40.868
did you know that
when a baby is born,

00:02:40.868 --> 00:02:43.334
they give him little kick
in the head?

00:02:43.334 --> 00:02:45.067
[ Laughter ]

00:02:47.167 --> 00:02:49.100
That's why they're like that.

00:02:49.100 --> 00:02:51.067
[ Laughter ]

00:02:55.567 --> 00:02:56.767
-I know when you're
out on the road,

00:02:56.767 --> 00:02:57.934
you do a lot of crowd work.

00:02:57.934 --> 00:02:59.834
Do you find out stuff
about people

00:02:59.834 --> 00:03:01.667
from interacting with audiences?

00:03:01.667 --> 00:03:03.801
-Yeah, it's probably
my favorite part,

00:03:03.801 --> 00:03:08.033
to do research and then --
yeah, and then find out more

00:03:08.033 --> 00:03:12.267
by talking with the people
in the crowd.

00:03:12.267 --> 00:03:14.133
Yeah, sometimes it's good.

00:03:14.133 --> 00:03:18.934
In Grand Rapids, probably
the drunkest city on tour.

00:03:18.934 --> 00:03:21.767
They were -- Some woman
was yelling at me

00:03:21.767 --> 00:03:24.534
to check out
a Frank Lloyd Wright house

00:03:24.534 --> 00:03:27.267
over and over again
and wouldn't stop.

00:03:27.267 --> 00:03:29.000
So I was yelling back.

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:32.234
I was saying, "You guys
used to make furniture.

00:03:32.234 --> 00:03:35.467
Now you make craft beer
as your big product.

00:03:35.467 --> 00:03:38.701
What happened?
What happened to you guys?"

00:03:38.701 --> 00:03:39.767
-So you were ready with that

00:03:39.767 --> 00:03:41.667
'cause you'd done
research about --

00:03:41.667 --> 00:03:44.200
[ Laughter ]

00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:46.667
-Yeah, they used to
make more furniture

00:03:46.667 --> 00:03:50.834
than almost any other place in
the country at a certain time.

00:03:50.834 --> 00:03:52.200
-Yeah.

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:54.801
-Yeah.

00:03:54.801 --> 00:03:56.834
I don't want to make them
feel bad or anything,

00:03:56.834 --> 00:03:58.167
but she wouldn't stop yelling

00:03:58.167 --> 00:03:59.901
about the Frank Lloyd Wright
house.

00:03:59.901 --> 00:04:01.100
-Yeah, so at some point,

00:04:01.100 --> 00:04:03.300
you got to give him
the hard truths

00:04:03.300 --> 00:04:06.467
about the falloff
in their furniture production.

00:04:06.467 --> 00:04:10.200
-I think we need
great furniture like this stuff.

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:11.634
-Oh.

00:04:11.634 --> 00:04:13.601
You're so -- so sweet.

00:04:13.601 --> 00:04:17.100
You -- Is it true that you
actually did crowd work

00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:19.801
with someone who had --
who had passed out?

00:04:19.801 --> 00:04:22.234
-Yeah, that happened in Aurora.

00:04:22.234 --> 00:04:24.267
I do some sleep material,

00:04:24.267 --> 00:04:26.067
so it wasn't like a --

00:04:26.067 --> 00:04:28.667
It's not out of the ordinary

00:04:28.667 --> 00:04:31.767
for them to fall asleep
for periods of time.

00:04:31.767 --> 00:04:35.367
But the guy was asleep
the entire show.

00:04:35.367 --> 00:04:37.400
-What do you think happens
when you go to a comedy show

00:04:37.400 --> 00:04:41.267
and fall asleep
right from the beginning?

00:04:41.267 --> 00:04:44.901
-I guess --
I mean, his case, he was...

00:04:44.901 --> 00:04:48.133
just drunk and didn't think
about the altitude in Colorado.

00:04:48.133 --> 00:04:49.734
I did -- I personally --

00:04:49.734 --> 00:04:52.567
I fell asleep at
Conner O'Malley's show one time

00:04:52.567 --> 00:04:54.167
'cause I had just moved
that day,

00:04:54.167 --> 00:04:56.200
but I wanted to go out
and support my friend.

00:04:56.200 --> 00:04:57.534
-Yeah.
-He actually --

00:04:57.534 --> 00:04:59.601
He broke character to say like,

00:04:59.601 --> 00:05:03.234
"Hey, man, you can go home
if you want to."

00:05:03.234 --> 00:05:04.901
-Your special is on YouTube,

00:05:04.901 --> 00:05:07.367
which is really exciting
'cause anyone can see it.

00:05:07.367 --> 00:05:10.000
But also, YouTube
obviously provides people

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:12.200
a chance to leave comments.

00:05:12.200 --> 00:05:15.834
Are you the kind of person who
would read the comment section?

00:05:15.834 --> 00:05:18.400
-It gets a little hairy,
and I try not to.

00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:19.868
-Yeah.

00:05:19.868 --> 00:05:24.334
-There was one comment
I did look at in Instagram.

00:05:24.334 --> 00:05:26.534
I put up the Dahmer joke
that you showed,

00:05:26.534 --> 00:05:31.467
and somebody said, "Thank you,
sir, for not doing murder."

00:05:31.467 --> 00:05:34.267
[ Laughter ]

00:05:34.267 --> 00:05:36.367
You know, ha-ha-ha-ha.

00:05:36.367 --> 00:05:40.100
But then a person
underneath said,

00:05:40.100 --> 00:05:44.767
"The dad in the joke is actually
upset because he's gay."

00:05:44.767 --> 00:05:47.434
And it's like, I didn't think --

00:05:47.434 --> 00:05:49.501
You know, I don't like
explaining jokes.

00:05:49.501 --> 00:05:52.234
That's not fun, and also,
you think that there's, like,

00:05:52.234 --> 00:05:56.100
a bottom level of intelligence
on the Internet,

00:05:56.100 --> 00:05:59.901
and, you know, and, yeah.

00:05:59.901 --> 00:06:03.834
So I just -- Yeah, it sucks
that you have to explain

00:06:03.834 --> 00:06:05.234
that that joke is about --

00:06:05.234 --> 00:06:07.701
-I mean, I'd love to give --
Yeah, it's about the murder.

00:06:07.701 --> 00:06:10.033
And just 'cause you're here,
where do you stand on murder?

00:06:10.033 --> 00:06:11.767
Just to clear it up, right.

00:06:11.767 --> 00:06:13.601
[ Laughter ]

00:06:15.767 --> 00:06:17.834
-It's not okay.
-Alright.

00:06:17.834 --> 00:06:20.300
[ Laughter ]
Yeah.

00:06:20.300 --> 00:06:22.968
A lot of times people
can get a little mealymouthed.

00:06:22.968 --> 00:06:24.567
It's nice that you came out
and just said it.

00:06:24.567 --> 00:06:28.000
-Thank you, yeah, and, no,
being gay is good, great even.

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:31.701
So, yeah, the joke is about
murdering and eating people.

00:06:31.701 --> 00:06:35.334
-Yeah, well, I think we can all
laugh at it a little bit more

00:06:35.334 --> 00:06:36.567
now that we know that.

00:06:36.567 --> 00:06:37.701
-I hope so.

00:06:37.701 --> 00:06:39.234
I just --
It never occurred to me

00:06:39.234 --> 00:06:40.801
that it could be taken that way.
-Yeah.

00:06:40.801 --> 00:06:42.334
Well, now we've cleared it up.

00:06:42.334 --> 00:06:44.200
And it's a wonderful special.

00:06:44.200 --> 00:06:45.601
And it's always so great
to have you back here.

00:06:45.601 --> 00:06:47.167
You guys, that's Joe Pera.

00:06:47.167 --> 00:06:49.000
"Slow &amp; Steady"
streaming on YouTube.

00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:52.801
We'll be right back
with Chef Ayesha Nurdjaja.

